@node D-Bus Methods
@section Method Reference

Reference for every method on @code{org.cmacs.Editor1} as of Phase 1.
D-Bus type signatures: @code{s} string, @code{i} int32, @code{u}
uint32, @code{x} int64, @code{t} uint64, @code{b} bool, @code{d}
double, @code{as} array-of-string, @code{v} variant, @code{o}
object-path, @code{h} file descriptor.

@subsection Eval

Evaluate an arbitrary elisp expression, return its @code{prin1} form.

@example
Eval (s expression) -> (s)
Errors: org.cmacs.Editor1.Error
@end example

@example
gdbus call --session --dest org.cmacs.Editor \
    --object-path /org/cmacs/Editor \
    --method org.cmacs.Editor1.Eval \
    '(format "%s on %s" (emacs-version) (system-name))'
# ('"GNU Emacs 31.0.50 ... on hostname"',)
@end example

This is the universal escape hatch.  Anything reachable from elisp
is reachable from here.

@subsection FindFile

Open a file or URL in the editor.

@example
FindFile (s path) -> ()

gdbus call --session --dest org.cmacs.Editor \
    --object-path /org/cmacs/Editor \
    --method org.cmacs.Editor1.FindFile \
    /etc/hostname
@end example

Equivalent to @code{M-x find-file}, so TRAMP paths work too.

@subsection Message

Display a message in the echo area.

@example
Message (s text) -> ()

gdbus call --session --dest org.cmacs.Editor \
    --object-path /org/cmacs/Editor \
    --method org.cmacs.Editor1.Message \
    "build complete"
@end example

@subsection GiRequire

Load a GObject Introspection namespace into cmacs's GI bridge.

@example
GiRequire (s namespace, s version) -> (b)

gdbus call --session --dest org.cmacs.Editor \
    --object-path /org/cmacs/Editor \
    --method org.cmacs.Editor1.GiRequire \
    GLib 2.0
# (true,)
@end example

@subsection GiCall

Call a function from a previously loaded GI namespace.  Each argument
is passed as a string and read on the cmacs side as a Lisp
expression --- bare words become strings, numbers pass through,
quoted strings stay quoted.

@example
GiCall (s namespace, s function, as args) -> (s)

gdbus call --session --dest org.cmacs.Editor \
    --object-path /org/cmacs/Editor \
    --method org.cmacs.Editor1.GiCall \
    GLib get_user_name '[]'
@end example

@subsection GiListFunctions

@example
GiListFunctions (s namespace) -> (as)

gdbus call --session --dest org.cmacs.Editor \
    --object-path /org/cmacs/Editor \
    --method org.cmacs.Editor1.GiListFunctions \
    GLib | head -3
@end example

@subsection Compositor methods (when --with-cmacs-gowl)

Bypass the elisp eval round-trip for compositor-control hot paths.
Each one returns a JSON-encoded string for structured fields and
@code{t}/@code{nil} strings for booleans.

These are on @strong{@code{org.cmacs.Editor1.Compositor}} at
@code{/org/cmacs/Editor}.  They used to live on
@code{org.cmacs.Editor1} with a @code{Gowl} prefix, and lost the prefix
when they moved, because the interface name now carries that context.
@code{GowlSetMfact} on @code{org.cmacs.Editor1} is @code{SetMfact} on
@code{org.cmacs.Editor1.Compositor}; the old names do not resolve.

@multitable @columnfractions .32 .22 .46
@headitem Method @tab Signature @tab Arguments / description
@item ListClients              @tab () -> (s)      @tab Every Wayland client, as JSON
@item FocusedClient            @tab () -> (s)      @tab The focused client, JSON or "nil"
@item FindClient               @tab (ss) -> (s)    @tab pattern, by ("title"/"app-id")
@item FocusClient              @tab (ss) -> (s)    @tab pattern, by
@item CloseClient              @tab (ss) -> (s)    @tab pattern, by
@item SetClientGeometry        @tab (ssiiii) -> (s) @tab pattern, by, x, y, width, height
@item Spawn                    @tab (s) -> (s)     @tab Launch a command with WAYLAND_DISPLAY set
@item SetLayout                @tab (s) -> (s)     @tab "tile" / "float" / "monocle"
@item SetMfact                 @tab (d) -> (s)     @tab Master-area split
@item SetNmaster               @tab (i) -> (s)     @tab Master-window count
@item ViewTags                 @tab (u) -> (s)     @tab Visible tag mask
@item WorkspaceList            @tab () -> (s)      @tab Workspaces, as JSON
@item WorkspaceSwitch          @tab (i) -> (s)     @tab Switch to workspace by id
@item AddRule                  @tab (ssubi) -> (s) @tab app_id, title, tags, floating, monitor
@item AddKeybind               @tab (sis) -> (s)   @tab key, action-id, arg
@item AddKeybindFull           @tab (siss) -> (s)  @tab key, action-id, arg, desc
@item RunKeybind               @tab (s) -> (s)     @tab Run what a key is bound to
@item ListKeybinds             @tab () -> (s)      @tab Bound keys with action nick, arg and desc
@item Lock / Unlock            @tab () -> (s)      @tab Toggle the session lock
@item Screenshot               @tab (ssss) -> (s)  @tab mode, client, by, file
@item ReloadConfig             @tab () -> (s)      @tab Reload the gowl config
@item ConfigGet                @tab (s) -> (s)     @tab Read a top-level config property
@item SetScreensaverWallpaper  @tab (s) -> (s)     @tab Animated screensaver wallpaper (config name)
@item StopScreensaverWallpaper @tab () -> (s)      @tab Stop the animated wallpaper
@item ListScreensaverConfigs   @tab () -> (s)      @tab List screensaver config names
@item ScreensaverStatus        @tab () -> (s)      @tab Renderer status
@item ScreensaverPause         @tab () -> (s)      @tab Pause the renderer
@item ScreensaverResume        @tab () -> (s)      @tab Resume the renderer
@item ScreensaverRestart       @tab () -> (s)      @tab Restart the renderer
@item ScreensaverSetFps        @tab (i) -> (s)     @tab Renderer frame rate
@end multitable

@code{AddKeybindFull} exists rather than a fourth argument on
@code{AddKeybind} because a D-Bus signature is part of the contract and
callers already use the @code{(sis)} form.  An empty @code{desc} means
none.

@code{RunKeybind} runs the action a key is bound to @emph{without} the
key being pressed.  This is not what the input-injection calls do:
those hand a key to the focused client and never consult the keybind
table.

@subsection Monitor methods (when --with-cmacs-gowl)

On @strong{@code{org.cmacs.Editor1.Monitor}}, split out of the
compositor block for the same reason: the interface name carries the
context, so the methods do not repeat it.
@code{GowlSetMonitorPosition} is @code{SetPosition} on
@code{org.cmacs.Editor1.Monitor}.

@multitable @columnfractions .22 .22 .56
@headitem Method @tab Signature @tab Arguments / description
@item List         @tab () -> (s)     @tab Attached monitors, as JSON
@item Info         @tab (s) -> (s)    @tab name -> full monitor info JSON
@item Modes        @tab (s) -> (s)    @tab name -> available modes JSON
@item Position     @tab (s) -> (s)    @tab name -> @{x, y@}
@item SetPosition  @tab (sii) -> (s)  @tab name, x, y
@item SetMode      @tab (siii) -> (s) @tab name, width, height, refresh-mHz
@item SetEnabled   @tab (sb) -> (s)   @tab name, enabled
@item SetScale     @tab (sd) -> (s)   @tab name, scale
@item SetTransform @tab (si) -> (s)   @tab name, 0..7
@end multitable

@example
# Resize the master area.
gdbus call --session --dest org.cmacs.Editor \
    --object-path /org/cmacs/Editor \
    --method org.cmacs.Editor1.Compositor.SetMfact 0.7

# Move the external monitor to (1920, 0) and disable the laptop panel.
gdbus call --session --dest org.cmacs.Editor \
    --object-path /org/cmacs/Editor \
    --method org.cmacs.Editor1.Monitor.SetPosition HDMI-A-1 1920 0
@end example

@noindent
The same surface is reachable as @command{emacsctl compositor ...} and
@command{emacsctl monitor ...}, which is usually less typing.

@subsection Error handling

Every method that takes elisp returns errors as
@code{org.cmacs.Editor1.Error} D-Bus errors.  In @command{gdbus}
they print as:

@example
Error: GDBus.Error:org.cmacs.Editor1.Error: ((wrong-number-of-arguments) ...)
@end example

Programmatic clients should match on the error name to decide
whether to surface to the user or retry.  Most languages' D-Bus
bindings turn the error into a typed exception.  See
@ref{D-Bus CLI Examples} for examples in each language.

@subsection org.cmacs.Editor1.Brigade

Drives AI agents.  Every argument and every return value is a string, so
a shell caller never has to think about D-Bus types; numeric arguments
are coerced on the far side.  All of them route through
@code{cmacs-brigade-call-tool}, the same entry point an agent reaches
over MCP---three surfaces, one code path.

@table @code
@item Spawn (task, agent, title, model) @result{} id
Hand a task to an agent.  All but the first argument may be empty.
@item Status (id), Result (id), Cancel (id), List ()
State and cost, what it produced, stop it, everything known.
@item Agents (), Providers (), Models (provider)
What can be spawned, and what it can be run against.
@item Send (id, message)
Queue a message.  Continues the existing conversation, so the agent
still remembers earlier turns; wakes a task that has already finished.
@item Inbox (id), Drop (id, index)
What is queued but not yet delivered, and removing one---an empty
@var{index} clears the queue.
@item Log (id, from_turn, kinds)
The transaction log: every message, reply and tool call.  Works on any
task, including ones the caller never spawned.  Both filters may be
empty.
@item Close (id)
Release the conversation's session.
@end table

Confirmation prompts are bypassed on this interface deliberately.  They
exist so an @emph{agent} cannot start paid work unattended; a D-Bus
caller is the session owner, who already has
@code{org.cmacs.Editor1.Eval} on the same bus.  It also could not work as
written: RPC evals bind @code{inhibit-interaction}, so a prompt inside a
dispatch signals rather than asking, and every @code{Spawn} would fail.

@example
busctl --user call org.cmacs.Editor /org/cmacs/Editor \
  org.cmacs.Editor1.Brigade Send ss "$ID" "now check the error paths"
@end example
